WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.318
The first lesson in M4 is about refusing to look at force by itself.

2
00:00:06.318 --> 00:00:12.636
A large push can be safe on one surface and damaging on another because the same push may be shared across many patches or crowded onto just a few.

3
00:00:12.636 --> 00:00:18.953
That is why pressure in solids is best read as patch load.

4
00:00:18.953 --> 00:00:25.271
The worked example becomes much easier when the learner stops asking only how big is the force and starts asking how much area is sharing it.

5
00:00:25.271 --> 00:00:31.589
Keep this takeaway in view: Solid pressure is a crowding story: the same push becomes more dangerous when it is packed into a smaller area.

6
00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:37.907
Same push means different patch load.

7
00:00:37.907 --> 00:00:44.224
A constant force can create different pressures if the contact area changes.

8
00:00:44.224 --> 00:00:50.542
Big idea means force per area.

9
00:00:50.542 --> 00:00:56.860
Pressure is not force alone; it is force spread across area.

10
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:03.521
This diagram matters because it turns a formula into a picture.

11
00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:10.182
One object pushes down with the same total force in both cases, but one footprint is narrow and the other is wide.

12
00:01:10.182 --> 00:01:16.844
The narrow case gives each patch a heavier load.

13
00:01:16.844 --> 00:01:23.505
The wide case shares the push more safely.

14
00:01:23.505 --> 00:01:30.166
Once students can see that difference, the pressure equation becomes almost obvious rather than mysterious.

15
00:01:30.166 --> 00:01:36.828
Keep this takeaway in view: A pressure diagram is strongest when students can point to where the same force is being shared differently.

16
00:01:36.828 --> 00:01:43.489
Narrow footprint: The same total push is crowded onto fewer square metres, so the pressure rises.

17
00:01:43.489 --> 00:01:50.150
Wide footprint: The push is spread more thinly, so the patch load falls.

18
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:54.861
A helpful analogy is snowshoes.

19
00:01:54.861 --> 00:01:59.572
The person's weight is the same either way, but a wider snowshoe spreads that weight across more snow.

20
00:01:59.572 --> 00:02:04.283
That reduces the patch load and makes sinking less likely.

21
00:02:04.283 --> 00:02:08.994
This analogy matters because it stops the lesson from feeling like a sterile equation.

22
00:02:08.994 --> 00:02:13.705
It shows why area belongs in the physical explanation.

23
00:02:13.705 --> 00:02:18.416
Keep this takeaway in view: The snowshoe picture makes pressure feel like load-sharing rather than like a random formula.

24
00:02:18.416 --> 00:02:23.127
Same person means same force.

25
00:02:23.127 --> 00:02:27.838
The downward push has not changed.

26
00:02:27.838 --> 00:02:32.549
Different shoe means different area.

27
00:02:32.549 --> 00:02:37.260
The contact area changes, so the pressure changes.

28
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:43.473
The core relationship is pressure equals force divided by area.

29
00:02:43.473 --> 00:02:49.687
That means the pressure rises if the force rises while the area stays fixed, and it rises if the area shrinks while the force stays fixed.

30
00:02:49.687 --> 00:02:55.900
The lesson becomes stronger when the student reads those comparison moves before calculating any number.

31
00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:02.113
Keep this takeaway in view: The pressure formula becomes much more useful when the student reads it as a load-sharing rule before substituting values.

32
00:03:02.113 --> 00:03:08.327
If force rises: Pressure rises when area stays the same.

33
00:03:08.327 --> 00:03:14.540
If area shrinks: Pressure rises when force stays the same.

34
00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:20.753
p = F / A.

35
00:03:20.753 --> 00:03:26.967
pressure in solids is force spread over area.

36
00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:33.180
Use the formula after deciding whether the story changes the force, the area, or both.

37
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.391
A reliable pattern for this lesson is to ask two questions.

38
00:03:37.391 --> 00:03:41.602
What is the total push?

39
00:03:41.602 --> 00:03:45.813
And how large is the footprint carrying it?

40
00:03:45.813 --> 00:03:50.024
Once those are clear, the pressure story is clear as well.

41
00:03:50.024 --> 00:03:54.235
This matters because many mistakes come from changing one quantity mentally while forgetting to track the other.

42
00:03:54.235 --> 00:03:58.446
Keep this takeaway in view: The patch-load routine keeps solid pressure questions conceptual instead of purely algebraic.

43
00:03:58.446 --> 00:04:02.657
Question one means how much push?.

44
00:04:02.657 --> 00:04:06.868
Identify the total force acting on the surface.

45
00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:11.079
Question two means how large a footprint?.

46
00:04:11.079 --> 00:04:15.290
Identify the contact area sharing that force.

47
00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:18.071
Try four checks.

48
00:04:18.071 --> 00:04:20.851
Is pressure force alone?

49
00:04:20.851 --> 00:04:23.632
No.

50
00:04:23.632 --> 00:04:26.412
If the same force is packed into a smaller area, what happens?

51
00:04:26.412 --> 00:04:29.193
Pressure rises.

52
00:04:29.193 --> 00:04:31.974
If the area doubles while force stays fixed, what happens?

53
00:04:31.974 --> 00:04:34.754
Pressure falls.

54
00:04:34.754 --> 00:04:37.535
And what does one pascal mean?

55
00:04:37.535 --> 00:04:40.316
One newton on each square metre.

56
00:04:40.316 --> 00:04:43.096
Those are the core ideas of the lesson.

57
00:04:43.096 --> 00:04:45.877
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: pressure in solids can now be read as a physical patch-load comparison.

58
00:04:45.877 --> 00:04:48.658
Pressure = force only?

59
00:04:48.658 --> 00:04:51.438
means no.

60
00:04:51.438 --> 00:04:54.219
Area belongs in the story as well.

61
00:04:54.219 --> 00:04:56.999
Smaller area means higher pressure.

62
00:04:56.999 --> 00:04:59.780
The push is more crowded onto each patch.

63
00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:05.409
You are ready for the worked example now.

64
00:05:05.409 --> 00:05:11.038
Start by identifying the total force and the contact area.

65
00:05:11.038 --> 00:05:16.667
Decide which one is changing.

66
00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:22.296
Then write the pressure relationship and calculate carefully.

67
00:05:22.296 --> 00:05:27.925
The best M4 Lesson 1 answers make it clear why the pressure changed, not just what number came out.

68
00:05:27.925 --> 00:05:33.555
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the student can now connect the formula to the footprint logic that makes it meaningful.

69
00:05:33.555 --> 00:05:39.184
Step 1: Read the force and the area as a pair.

70
00:05:39.184 --> 00:05:44.813
Step 2: Keep the unit of pressure visible as pascals or N/m^2.

71
00:05:44.813 --> 00:05:50.442
identify the total push.

72
00:05:50.442 --> 00:05:56.071
identify the footprint carrying it.

73
00:05:56.071 --> 00:06:01.700
Strong answers explain whether the pressure changed because the push changed, the area changed, or both.
